WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.080
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.260
Hey Crazies.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.240
Figuring out what’s going on with quantum particles can be a real hassle sometimes,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.100
mainly because their behavior works against us.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.100
You’ve probably heard of the infamous double-slit experiment

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.900
because everyone and their mother has done a video on it.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.000
But most of those videos are designed to mystify you.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.700
Let’s bring quantum mechanics back to reality.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.360
Whenever you’re trying to learn something about quantum particles,

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.480
never forget the following maxim:

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.460
Quantum mechanics is not magic!

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.420
There are real physical things happening here that are independent of human existence.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.660
We just need to figure out what they are.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.280
The double-slit experiment is a good place to start.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.440
It’s an example of something we call a “which way” experiment

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.700
where all we want to do is determine which way something went.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.860
If we know it started here and ended here,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.000
did it take this path? Or that one?

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.520
Or some other weird path?

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.080
Honestly, it’s a pretty common question to ask about anything physical.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.000
Being able to make predictions is kind of a big deal.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.040
Adding in barriers is a perfect way to narrow down the options and focus our attention.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.980
As we move forward in this video,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.500
we’re going to ask progressively deeper questions about this experiment,

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.860
about what its results actually mean.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.180
We’re going to add pieces to it as we go until it looks like this:

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.340
the delayed-choice quantum eraser.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.240
That’s when we’ll find the deeper answers, but we have to work up to it slowly.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.560
So settle in!

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.020
In a previous video, we did the double-slit experiment with a full beam of light.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.380
If you’re not familiar with that experiment or how wave interference works,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.860
you should go watch this video first and come back after.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.400
Ok, let’s keep going.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.520
We want to take the double-slit experiment into the quantum realm

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.520
and no, it doesn’t look like this at all.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.530
In fact, it doesn’t really look like anything

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:52.020
because light doesn’t work the same way on that level.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.120
Down there, light is made of individual photons.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.320
What’s a photon?

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.180
The smallest piece of energy in a beam of light.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.260
We’ve spent a lot of time modeling light as a wave in the electromagnetic field.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:07.120
But, that’s not always the best model, especially for a laser like this.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.100
If we make it really dim, the beam stops being continuous.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.200
We start to see individual packets of energy called photons.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.219
If we make that laser dim enough, it’ll release one photon at a time.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.320
Then we can send each one into the double-slit experiment.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.659
Every time a photon hits the screen, it records the interaction as a dot.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.740
At first, everything looks fine.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.960
Individual photons are making individual dots.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.320
After a while, you’ll notice a pattern build up on the screen.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:36.900
It’s an interference pattern.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.720
The first thing this result tells us is that the photon is a wave.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.520
Wait wait wait! Isn’t the photon a particle?

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.660
Yes, but not in the way you’re probably thinking.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.599
When you hear the word “particle” you probably think of something like dust.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.000
It gives you the impression that quantum particles like photons are little balls,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.080
but they’re not.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.100
Quantum particles are just localized waves.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.840
At all times, their behavior is described by a wave function,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.420
which is a solution to a wave equation.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.320
They are always waves!

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.620
A pattern like this in the double-slit experiment tells us there is interference

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.820
and a wave is the only thing that can interfere like that.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.180
Just to be clear though.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.460
While the interference pattern tells you the thing is a wave.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.740
The non-interference pattern does not tell you the opposite.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.320
This pattern does not mean “particle.”

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.240
It just means we’re inconclusive about waviness.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.780
So, photons are waves, but their tininess can give them some semblance of location

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.120
and that’s going to be very important as we try to understand their behavior.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.900
In general, this isn’t a waviness test or a particle test.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.480
It’s a “which way” test.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.500
The point is to test “which way” something went.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.980
Did it go through slit A, slit B, or both slits simultaneously?

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:54.480
That’s the second thing our photon result from earlier can tell us.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.439
The only way this interference pattern is possible

00:03:57.439 --> 00:04:01.909
is if each individual photon went through both slits simultaneously.

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:04.340
Seriously! Both slits!

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:05.640
How is that possible?

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.380
Well, if each photon is actually a wave, then this isn’t all that surprising.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.040
But what if we try to detect which slit each photon passed through?

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.980
Sure, we could do that, but then we get a different result.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.280
Hmm...

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.180
Polarizers do a pretty good job of separating photons.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.140
Just after slit A, we’ll put in a horizontal polarizer

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.260
and just after slit B, we’ll put in a vertical polarizer.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.040
That way we’ll be able to know which photon came from which slit.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.480
Unfortunately, now the pattern looks like this instead.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.000
No interference.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.560
Doesn’t that mean light is a particle?

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.320
Dude, keep up! We just went over this.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:42.560
While the interference pattern tells you the thing is a wave.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.920
The non-interference pattern does not tell you the opposite.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.480
This pattern does not mean “particle.”

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.320
It just means we’re inconclusive about waviness.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.840
But it doesn’t matter if we’re inclusive this time.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.150
The previous experiment told us photons were waves already.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.220
This new result tells us something else entirely.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.300
It tells us the detection of each photon changed their behavior.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:09.020
Now each photon only goes through one slit or the other, not both.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.020
The photons knew we were watching?!

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.070
No, that’s not how this works.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:15.100
That’s not how any of this works!

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.280
Remember, quantum mechanics is not magic!

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:22.180
I never said any person knew which slit each photon went through.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.600
I only said they were able to know.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.480
Each photon interacted with the polarizers.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.760
That interaction is what changed the behavior of the photons, not the person.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:34.120
It made the slit information available.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:38.920
Whether or not a person actually took advantage of that availability is completely irrelevant.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:40.580
The humans are not important!

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.220
So, then, what’s actually happening?

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:43.820
Probability is happening.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.760
All the photons come out of the same hole in the laser.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.200
Each photon could exit from anywhere along its diameter,

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.220
but they’re more likely to come out of the center than the edge,

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.300
so the photon’s quantum wave looks like this.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:01.620
The height of that wave gives us some idea of where we might find the photon at any given moment.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.260
Eventually, that wave encounters something like our slit wall.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:08.920
Most of the photons localize on the barrier and are destroyed,

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.360
but some of them localize on the openings and pass through.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:16.880
Each photon wave that passes through has both slits available to it.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.540
They’re either detected by the wall and localize in one area or they pass through both openings.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:28.040
That turns the slits into two independent sources of probability that can interfere with each other.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.840
The new wave looks something like this on the opposite side of the slits.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.920
It spreads out as it travels until the photon localizes on the screen.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.720
It gets destroyed, its energy is transfer, and we see a dot.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:45.200
Those dots have size because it’s impossible to localize a particle to a single point.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.860
You know, the uncertainty principle and all that jazz.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:52.040
Over time, those dots build up on the screen and eventually form a pattern.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:56.540
Since each photon localizes randomly based on this interference wave.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:59.320
That pattern on the screen is an interference pattern.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.200
What about when we did all that detecting?

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.860
There’s an extra interaction.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:07.680
The polarizers force each photon to take a particular polarization.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.660
Since each slit has a different polarizer, each photon can only pass through one of them.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:16.540
The new wave looks like this on the opposite side of the polarizers

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:20.380
and that remaining wave peak travels to the screen and deposits a dot.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.160
But, that’s a different probability wave arriving at the screen,

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.700
which means we build up a different pattern over time.

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:29.600
The extra interaction with the polarizers prevented the interference.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.180
Quantum mechanics is not magic!

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.640
But what about the delayed-choice quantum eraser?

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:37.520
Ok, fine. It’s time for that anyway.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.880
But, just so you know, the same rules apply.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.800
The best way to test a hypothesis is to try and prove it wrong.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:48.220
Is there any way we can delay the detection until after the photon hits the screen?

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:50.950
Yes, but we need to be a little more creative.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:54.260
The photon that hits the screen is immediately destroyed upon impact,

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:57.540
so the first thing we need to do is copy the photon.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.780
If we put a special kind of crystal immediately after the slits,

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.020
every photon will be split into an entangled pair of photons,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:08.240
each photon having half the energy of the original.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.280
Conservation of energy shall not be violated!

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.740
After that, we need to split up the partners.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.940
A special kind of prism should do the trick.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:17.780
One partner goes off to the screen.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.880
The other partner goes off to some detectors.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:24.240
Detector A is for Slit A and Detector B is for Slit B.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.980
By separating the two partners, we can make the detection happen whenever we want.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.280
We could do it immediately

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:33.400
and it would be just like the previous experiment with the polarizers.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.080
But, if we do it way later,

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:40.280
the upper photon will hit the screen and make a dot before we detect the slit information.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:42.300
What kind of pattern do we get then?

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:46.360
Just like before, if we detect the photons, we don’t get interference.

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:47.460
For real!

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.980
It doesn’t matter when the detection is made.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.960
All that seems to matter is that the information will become available.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:56.520
Hmm, let’s take this up a notch.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.780
If we add in a beam splitter before each of our detectors,

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:02.600
only half of the photons end up getting detected.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:05.920
The other half pass through and we can send them somewhere else.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.400
Why would we want to do that?

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:09.400
To erase the information.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:13.860
We can send that half of the photons into a box called the Quantum Eraser.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:18.720
If a photon is detected by that box, we have no idea which slit it came from.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.320
That information has effectively been erased from existence.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.200
This means we’re only collecting slit information from half of the photons.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:29.620
In this case, the pattern on the screen looks like a big mess.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:33.020
But don’t forget, we’re still only sending in photons one at a time,

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.980
which should let us keep a record of which hits are entangled pairs.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.400
When we use that information to separate the dots on the screen,

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.880
we get interference for the erased half

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:45.500
and no interference for the detected half.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.040
By performing all these detections and erasures much later,

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.260
it almost seems like a photon gets detected and is like

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:53.820
“Oh crap! I’m detected!”

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.880
Then it somehow communicates that to its entangled partner in the past,

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.920
so it’ll land without interference.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:01.220
Get a hold of yourself Nick!

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.020
Quantum mechanics is not magic!

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.540
Ok, reality check.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:10.440
This last experiment, is what we call the “delay-choice quantum eraser”

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:12.800
and it's rather elaborate and convoluted.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:15.780
If we jump to too many conclusions about this kind of result

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:20.040
we’ll have to subscribe to a particular interpretation of quantum mechanics.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:22.080
There’s a whole bunch of these interpretations.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.540
Some have been ruled out, but some pretty crazy ones still remain.

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:27.440
Let’s not go down that rabbit hole today.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:30.280
How about a more rational perspective?

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:34.160
We spent so much time focusing on the delayed detectors over here

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:37.760
that we didn’t stop to consider the screen is also a detection.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.160
You could just as easily say the pattern on the wall

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:42.740
is what determines what happens at the other detectors.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:46.260
Whichever detection happens first determines the other detection.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:50.360
I mean, the pattern can’t even be seen until all the detections have been made anyway.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:55.740
Quantum mechanics is a physical model that allows us to make predictions about really tiny things.

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:57.260
Maybe that’s all it is.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:01.000
Maybe elaborate experiments like the delay-choice quantum eraser

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:03.820
just expose the limits of our physical models.

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:07.860
In quantum mechanics, maybe the rational perspective is the craziest.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.230
So, do you feel any better about quantum mechanics?

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:11.670
Let us know in the comments.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:13.280
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:15.560
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:21.300
A special thanks goes out to Patreon patrons like Matthew O'Connor, Eoin O’Sullivan, I'mKorgThisIsMiek

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:24.600
who help make this show possible with their incredibly generous support.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.280
And until next time, remember, it’s ok to be a little crazy.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.180
Doesn’t the photoelectric effect prove the photon is a particle?

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:33.880
Nope!

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:38.060
The photoelectric effect is a specific case of the energy conservation principle.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.640
The photon is a discrete packet of energy.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:42.960
That discrete packet just happens to be a wave.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:44.540
Thanks for watching!

